---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { loadContent } from "../../lib/content";
import { calBookingUrl } from "../../lib/site";

const { provider } = loadContent();
---

<Layout
  title={`The Rise of Nationalism in Europe | CBSE Class 10 History | ${provider.name}`}
  description="CBSE Class 10 History Chapter 1 study notes on The Rise of Nationalism in Europe. French Revolution, Napoleonic Code 1804, Zollverein, Mazzini, Unification of Germany and Italy, Balkan crisis, visual diagrams, and an interactive 20-question board quiz."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Rise of Nationalism in Europe
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Social Science · History Chapter 1</span>
      <h1>The Rise of Nationalism in Europe</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Comprehensive revision notes, timelines, comparative analysis of German and Italian unifications, allegories, and an interactive 20-question board exam quiz covering 100% of CBSE Class 10 History.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Key Historical Concepts &amp; Chronology</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🇫🇷 French Revolution &amp; Idea of the Nation</h3>
          <p class="formula">1789: Sovereignty transferred from Monarchy to French Citizens</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Sense of Collective Identity:</strong> Ideas of <em>la patrie</em> (the fatherland) and <em>le citoyen</em> (the citizen); a new French tricolour flag replaced the former royal standard.</li>
            <li><strong>Administrative Reforms:</strong> National Assembly elected by active citizens; centralized administrative system; abolition of internal customs duties; uniform system of weights and measures; French adopted as the national lingua franca.</li>
            <li><strong>Civil Code of 1804 (Napoleonic Code):</strong>
              <br/>• Abolished all privileges based on birth.
              <br/>• Established equality before the law and secured the right to property.
              <br/>• Abolished feudal system and freed peasants from serfdom and manorial dues.
              <br/>• Standardized currency, weights, and transport in Dutch Republic, Switzerland, Italy, and Germany.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚖️ Liberal Nationalism &amp; Zollverein</h3>
          <p class="formula">Liberty, Equality &amp; Economic Freedom</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Political Liberalism:</strong> Freedom for the individual, equality of all before the law, government by consent, end of autocracy and clerical privileges, and a constitution with representative government.</li>
            <li><strong>Economic Liberalism:</strong> Freedom of markets and abolition of state-imposed restrictions on movement of goods and capital.</li>
            <li><strong>Zollverein (1834):</strong> A customs union formed at the initiative of Prussia and joined by most German states. It abolished tariff barriers and reduced the number of currencies from over thirty to two, creating national economic unity.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>👑 Conservatism &amp; The Treaty of Vienna (1815)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Defeat of Napoleon &amp; Restoration of Monarchies</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>Following Napoleon's defeat at Waterloo (1815), Britain, Russia, Prussia, and Austria hosted the Congress of Vienna chaired by Austrian Chancellor <strong>Duke Metternich</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Key Decisions:</strong>
              <br/>• Bourbon dynasty restored to power in France.
              <br/>• A series of buffer states set up on French borders to prevent future French expansion (Kingdom of Netherlands included Belgium; Genoa added to Piedmont).
              <br/>• Prussia given new territories on its western frontiers; Austria gained control of northern Italy.
              <br/>• The German Confederation of 39 states set up by Napoleon was left untouched.
            </li>
            <li>Conservative regimes were autocratic, intolerant of criticism, and imposed strict <strong>censorship</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🗡️ Giuseppe Mazzini &amp; Secret Societies</h3>
          <p class="formula">"God had intended nations to be the natural units of mankind"</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>Italian revolutionary born in Genoa in 1807; member of the secret society of the <strong>Carbonari</strong>.</li>
            <li>Exiled in 1831 for attempting a revolution in Liguria.</li>
            <li>Founded two influential underground societies:
              <br/>1. <strong>Young Italy</strong> in Marseilles (1831)
              <br/>2. <strong>Young Europe</strong> in Berne (1833)
            </li>
            <li>Believed in a unified Italian Republic. Metternich described him as <em>"the most dangerous enemy of our social order."</em></li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: Unification of Germany vs. Italy</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 1 · Unification of Germany (1866–1871)</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Unification of Germany process">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Title -->
              <text x="230" y="24" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#0f766e">Architect: Otto von Bismarck (Prussian Chief Minister)</text>

              <!-- Step 1: Frankfurt Parliament Fail -->
              <rect x="20" y="45" width="120" height="60" rx="6" fill="#fee2e2" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="80" y="68" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#991b1b">1848 Initiative</text>
              <text x="80" y="85" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#7f1d1d">Frankfurt Parliament</text>
              <text x="80" y="98" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#b91c1c">Repressed by Monarchy</text>

              <!-- Arrow 1 -->
              <line x1="140" y1="75" x2="170" y2="75" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>

              <!-- Step 2: Policy of Blood and Iron -->
              <rect x="170" y="45" width="120" height="60" rx="6" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="230" y="68" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#92400e">Blood &amp; Iron</text>
              <text x="230" y="85" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#78350f">Bismarck &amp; Prussian Army</text>
              <text x="230" y="98" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#b45309">Supported by Junkers</text>

              <!-- Arrow 2 -->
              <line x1="290" y1="75" x2="320" y2="75" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/>

              <!-- Step 3: Three Wars over 7 Years -->
              <rect x="320" y="45" width="120" height="60" rx="6" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#0284c7" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="380" y="68" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#0369a1">3 Wars (7 Years)</text>
              <text x="380" y="85" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#075985">vs Austria, Denmark</text>
              <text x="380" y="98" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#0369a1">&amp; France (Prussia Wins)</text>

              <!-- Final Unification Milestone Banner -->
              <rect x="40" y="130" width="380" height="85" rx="8" fill="#ecfdf5" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2"/>
              <text x="230" y="155" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="#065f46">January 1871 · Hall of Mirrors, Versailles</text>
              <text x="230" y="175" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#047857">Prussian King Kaiser William I proclaimed German Emperor</text>
              <text x="230" y="195" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="600" fill="#0f766e">Modernized currency, banking, legal, and judicial systems in Germany</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="visual-caption">Prussia used military leadership under Bismarck to fight three wars over 7 years to unite Germany.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 2 · Unification of Italy: The 4 Leaders</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Italian unification leaders and regions">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- 4 Quadrants: Mazzini, Cavour, Garibaldi, Victor Emmanuel II -->
              <!-- 1. Mazzini -->
              <rect x="20" y="20" width="195" height="90" rx="6" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#0284c7" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="35" y="42" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#0369a1">Giuseppe Mazzini (The Soul)</text>
              <text x="35" y="60" font-size="9" fill="#334155">• Secret societies: Young Italy</text>
              <text x="35" y="75" font-size="9" fill="#334155">• Ideological foundation for unified</text>
              <text x="35" y="90" font-size="9" fill="#334155">  democratic republic</text>

              <!-- 2. Cavour -->
              <rect x="245" y="20" width="195" height="90" rx="6" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#0f766e" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="260" y="42" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#0f766e">Count Cavour (The Brain)</text>
              <text x="260" y="60" font-size="9" fill="#334155">• Chief Minister of Piedmont-Sardinia</text>
              <text x="260" y="75" font-size="9" fill="#334155">• Tactful French diplomatic alliance (1859)</text>
              <text x="260" y="90" font-size="9" fill="#334155">• Defeated Austrian forces in North</text>

              <!-- 3. Garibaldi -->
              <rect x="20" y="125" width="195" height="95" rx="6" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#dc2626" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="35" y="147" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#b91c1c">Giuseppe Garibaldi (The Sword)</text>
              <text x="35" y="165" font-size="9" fill="#334155">• Led armed volunteers ("Red Shirts")</text>
              <text x="35" y="180" font-size="9" fill="#334155">• 1860: Expedition of the Thousand into</text>
              <text x="35" y="195" font-size="9" fill="#334155">  Two Sicilies; won local peasant support</text>

              <!-- 4. Victor Emmanuel II -->
              <rect x="245" y="125" width="195" height="95" rx="6" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="260" y="147" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#b45309">Victor Emmanuel II (The Crown)</text>
              <text x="260" y="165" font-size="9" fill="#334155">• King of Sardinia-Piedmont</text>
              <text x="260" y="185" font-size="10" font-weight="700" fill="#92400e">1861: Proclaimed King of United Italy</text>
              <text x="260" y="202" font-size="9" fill="#78350f">Papal States annexed in 1870</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="visual-caption">Italy had been fragmented into 7 states; only Sardinia-Piedmont was ruled by an Italian princely house.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visualising the Nation & Balkans -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>National Allegories &amp; The Balkan Crisis</h2>
      <div class="grid two">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🏛️ Female Allegories of the Nation</h3>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Marianne (France):</strong> Represented the Republic; characterized by the red cap, tricolour, and cockade. Statues were erected in public squares to remind the public of the national symbol of unity.</li>
            <li><strong>Germania (Germany):</strong> Visual representation of the German nation. Wears a crown of <strong>oak leaves</strong>, as the German oak stands for heroism.</li>
            <li><strong>Symbols:</strong> Broken chains = Being freed; Breastplate with eagle = Symbol of German empire and strength; Sword = Readiness to fight; Olive branch around sword = Willingness to make peace; Black, red, and gold tricolour = Flag of liberal nationalists in 1848.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>💥 The Balkan Powder Keg &amp; Imperialism</h3>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Balkans:</strong> Region of geographical and ethnic variation comprising modern Romania, Bulgaria, Albania, Greece, Macedonia, Croatia, Bosnia-Herzegovina, Slovenia, Serbia, and Montenegro. Inhabitants were broadly known as the <strong>Slavs</strong>.</li>
            <li>Large part under Ottoman Empire control. Disintegration of Ottoman Empire and spread of romantic nationalism made the region explosive.</li>
            <li>Balkan states were fiercely jealous of each other and each hoped to gain territory at the expense of others.</li>
            <li><strong>Big Power Rivalry:</strong> Russia, Germany, England, and Austro-Hungary competed for trade, colonies, naval, and military dominance in the Balkans, ultimately triggering the <strong>First World War (1914)</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Interactive 20-Question Quiz -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Interactive CBSE Board Exam Quiz (20 Questions)</h2>
      <p class="muted">Test your knowledge with authentic CBSE Class 10 board exam MCQs. Instant feedback and explanations included.</p>

      <div class="quiz-container card" id="quiz-box">
        <div class="quiz-header-bar">
          <span class="quiz-counter" id="quiz-counter">Question 1 of 20</span>
          <span class="quiz-score" id="quiz-score">Score: 0 / 20</span>
        </div>

        <div class="quiz-question-body">
          <p class="quiz-q-text" id="quiz-question">Loading question...</p>
          <div class="quiz-options-list" id="quiz-options"></div>
          <div class="quiz-feedback" id="quiz-feedback"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="quiz-footer-actions">
          <button class="button secondary" id="quiz-prev-btn" disabled>← Previous</button>
          <button class="button" id="quiz-next-btn" disabled>Next Question →</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-completed-banner card" id="quiz-result-box" style="display:none; text-align:center; padding:2.5rem;">
        <h3>Quiz Completed! 🎉</h3>
        <p class="quiz-final-score" id="quiz-final-score" style="font-size:1.4rem; font-weight:700; color:var(--brand); margin:1rem 0;"></p>
        <p id="quiz-final-msg" class="muted"></p>
        <button class="button" id="quiz-restart-btn" style="margin-top:1rem;">Retake Quiz</button>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="cta-banner">
      <h2>Need personalized guidance for Class 10 Social Science?</h2>
      <p>Book a free {provider.trial.duration_minutes}-minute trial session with {provider.tutor_name} (online or at-home) to master answer-writing frameworks and score 100% in CBSE Board Exams.</p>
      <a class="button" href={calBookingUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Book Free Trial</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
(function() {
  const quizData = [
    {
      q: "Who was the French artist who prepared a series of four prints visualising a world made up of 'democratic and social Republics' in 1848?",
      options: ["Frédéric Sorrieu", "Claude Monet", "Eugène Delacroix", "Louis Philippe"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "In 1848, Frédéric Sorrieu, a French artist, prepared a series of four prints visualising his dream of a world made up of democratic and social Republics."
    },
    {
      q: "Which of the following was NOT a feature of the Napoleonic Code of 1804?",
      options: ["Abolished privileges based on birth", "Established equality before law", "Secured right to property", "Restored the feudal system"],
      answer: 3,
      explanation: "The Civil Code of 1804 (Napoleonic Code) actually abolished the feudal system, freed peasants from serfdom, and removed birth privileges. It did not restore feudalism."
    },
    {
      q: "What was the main objective of the Zollverein formed in 1834?",
      options: ["To suppress democratic movements", "To abolish tariff barriers and reduce currencies", "To enforce German language", "To partition Austria"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Zollverein was a customs union formed at the initiative of Prussia that abolished internal tariff barriers and reduced the number of currencies from over thirty to two."
    },
    {
      q: "Which Treaty recognized Greece as an independent nation in 1832?",
      options: ["Treaty of Vienna", "Treaty of Versailles", "Treaty of Constantinople", "Treaty of Paris"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "The Treaty of Constantinople of 1832 recognized Greece as an independent nation following the Greek War of Independence against the Ottoman Empire."
    },
    {
      q: "Who remarked: 'When France sneezes, the rest of Europe catches cold'?",
      options: ["Giuseppe Mazzini", "Duke Metternich", "Otto von Bismarck", "Napoleon Bonaparte"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Austrian Chancellor Duke Metternich famously made this remark referring to how revolutionary events in France consistently sparked chain reactions across Europe."
    },
    {
      q: "Who founded the secret society called 'Young Italy'?",
      options: ["Count Cavour", "Giuseppe Garibaldi", "Giuseppe Mazzini", "Victor Emmanuel II"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "Giuseppe Mazzini founded 'Young Italy' in Marseilles (1831) and 'Young Europe' in Berne (1833) to promote Italian unification."
    },
    {
      q: "The Frankfurt Parliament was convened in the year 1848 in the Church of:",
      options: ["St. Paul", "St. Peter", "St. Mark", "St. Nicholas"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "On 18 May 1848, 831 elected representatives marched in a festive procession to take their places in the Frankfurt Parliament convened in the Church of St. Paul."
    },
    {
      q: "Who was the chief architect of the unification of Germany?",
      options: ["Kaiser William I", "Otto von Bismarck", "Hitler", "Metternich"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Prussian Chief Minister Otto von Bismarck carried out the unification process with the help of the Prussian army and bureaucracy through his 'Blood and Iron' policy."
    },
    {
      q: "In which year was the Prussian King Kaiser William I proclaimed German Emperor at Versailles?",
      options: ["1861", "1871", "1885", "1848"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "In January 1871, an assembly of princes, army representatives, and Bismarck gathered in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles to proclaim Kaiser William I as German Emperor."
    },
    {
      q: "Which state was the only Italian state ruled by an Italian princely house before unification?",
      options: ["Kingdom of the Two Sicilies", "Lombardy-Venetia", "Sardinia-Piedmont", "Papal States"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "Out of the seven fragmented Italian states, only Sardinia-Piedmont was ruled by an Italian princely house (King Victor Emmanuel II)."
    },
    {
      q: "Who led the famous 'Expedition of the Thousand' to South Italy in 1860?",
      options: ["Giuseppe Garibaldi", "Giuseppe Mazzini", "Count Cavour", "Victor Emmanuel II"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "Giuseppe Garibaldi and his armed volunteer force ('Red Shirts') marched into South Italy and the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies in 1860, driving out the Spanish Bourbon rulers."
    },
    {
      q: "What does the crown of oak leaves worn by the allegory 'Germania' stand for?",
      options: ["Peace", "Readiness to fight", "Heroism", "Willingness to make peace"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "Germania wears a crown of oak leaves because the German oak is a traditional symbol of heroism."
    },
    {
      q: "The Act of Union 1707 between England and Scotland resulted in the formation of:",
      options: ["United Kingdom of Great Britain", "British Commonwealth", "British Empire", "United States of Europe"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "The Act of Union (1707) between England and Scotland resulted in the formation of the 'United Kingdom of Great Britain' and gave England influence over Scotland."
    },
    {
      q: "What does a broken chain symbolize in national allegories?",
      options: ["Being freed", "Heroism", "Readiness to fight", "Beginning of a new era"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "Broken chains symbolized liberation and being freed from monarchical subjugation and serfdom."
    },
    {
      q: "The inhabitants of the vast Balkan region under the Ottoman Empire were broadly known as:",
      options: ["Slavs", "Nordics", "Celts", "Turks"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "The inhabitants of the Balkan region (comprising modern Romania, Bulgaria, Serbia, Greece, etc.) were broadly known as the Slavs."
    },
    {
      q: "Which European powers were in intense rivalry over trade and naval dominance in the Balkans leading to WW1?",
      options: ["Russia, Germany, England, Austro-Hungary", "USA, Japan, Russia, China", "Spain, Portugal, Italy, Greece", "Ottoman Empire, Egypt, Persia"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "The major powers competing for dominance over colonies, trade, and territories in the Balkans were Russia, Germany, England, and Austro-Hungary."
    },
    {
      q: "Who was proclaimed the King of United Italy in 1861?",
      options: ["Victor Emmanuel II", "Count Cavour", "Garibaldi", "Mazzini"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "In 1861, Victor Emmanuel II was proclaimed King of United Italy."
    },
    {
      q: "What was the primary goal of the Treaty of Vienna of 1815?",
      options: ["To establish democracies across Europe", "To restore the monarchies overthrown by Napoleon", "To encourage German unification", "To support Italian independence"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "The primary objective of the Congress of Vienna was to undo most of the changes brought about by the Napoleonic wars and restore conservative monarchical regimes."
    },
    {
      q: "Romanticism was a cultural movement that sought to develop a particular form of:",
      options: ["Nationalist sentiment through art, poetry, and folk culture", "Industrial technology", "Military conquest", "Religious fanaticism"],
      answer: 0,
      explanation: "Romanticism focused on emotions, intuition, folk music, language, and mystical feelings to shape a shared collective heritage and nationalist sentiment."
    },
    {
      q: "The allegory of France was given the popular Christian name:",
      options: ["Germania", "Marianne", "Britannia", "Columbia"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "The female allegory of France was named Marianne, an ordinary Christian name symbolizing the people's nation with characteristics of Liberty and the Republic."
    }
  ];

  let currentIdx = 0;
  let userAnswers = new Array(quizData.length).fill(null);

  const qText = document.getElementById("quiz-question");
  const optionsBox = document.getElementById("quiz-options");
  const feedbackBox = document.getElementById("quiz-feedback");
  const counterBox = document.getElementById("quiz-counter");
  const scoreBox = document.getElementById("quiz-score");
  const prevBtn = document.getElementById("quiz-prev-btn");
  const nextBtn = document.getElementById("quiz-next-btn");
  const quizBox = document.getElementById("quiz-box");
  const resultBox = document.getElementById("quiz-result-box");
  const finalScore = document.getElementById("quiz-final-score");
  const finalMsg = document.getElementById("quiz-final-msg");
  const restartBtn = document.getElementById("quiz-restart-btn");

  function renderQuestion(idx) {
    const item = quizData[idx];
    counterBox.textContent = `Question ${idx + 1} of ${quizData.length}`;
    qText.textContent = item.q;
    optionsBox.innerHTML = "";
    feedbackBox.textContent = "";
    feedbackBox.className = "quiz-feedback";

    const selected = userAnswers[idx];

    item.options.forEach((optText, oIdx) => {
      const btn = document.createElement("button");
      btn.className = "quiz-opt-btn";
      btn.textContent = `${String.fromCharCode(65 + oIdx)}. ${optText}`;

      if (selected !== null) {
        btn.disabled = true;
        if (oIdx === item.answer) {
          btn.classList.add("opt-correct");
        } else if (oIdx === selected) {
          btn.classList.add("opt-wrong");
        }
      } else {
        btn.onclick = () => selectOption(oIdx);
      }

      optionsBox.appendChild(btn);
    });

    if (selected !== null) {
      if (selected === item.answer) {
        feedbackBox.textContent = `✓ Correct! ${item.explanation}`;
        feedbackBox.classList.add("feedback-correct");
      } else {
        feedbackBox.textContent = `✗ Incorrect. Correct answer is option ${String.fromCharCode(65 + item.answer)}. ${item.explanation}`;
        feedbackBox.classList.add("feedback-wrong");
      }
    }

    prevBtn.disabled = idx === 0;
    nextBtn.disabled = selected === null;
    nextBtn.textContent = idx === quizData.length - 1 ? "Finish Quiz →" : "Next Question →";
    updateScoreDisplay();
  }

  function selectOption(oIdx) {
    userAnswers[currentIdx] = oIdx;
    renderQuestion(currentIdx);
  }

  function updateScoreDisplay() {
    let score = 0;
    userAnswers.forEach((ans, i) => {
      if (ans === quizData[i].answer) score++;
    });
    scoreBox.textContent = `Score: ${score} / ${quizData.length}`;
  }

  prevBtn.onclick = () => {
    if (currentIdx > 0) {
      currentIdx--;
      renderQuestion(currentIdx);
    }
  };

  nextBtn.onclick = () => {
    if (currentIdx < quizData.length - 1) {
      currentIdx++;
      renderQuestion(currentIdx);
    } else {
      finishQuiz();
    }
  };

  function finishQuiz() {
    quizBox.style.display = "none";
    resultBox.style.display = "block";
    let score = 0;
    userAnswers.forEach((ans, i) => {
      if (ans === quizData[i].answer) score++;
    });
    finalScore.textContent = `Your Final Score: ${score} / ${quizData.length} (${Math.round((score / quizData.length) * 100)}%)`;
    if (score >= 18) {
      finalMsg.textContent = "Superb mastery! You have thorough command over Nationalism in Europe, unifications, and historical chronologies.";
    } else if (score >= 14) {
      finalMsg.textContent = "Great job! Review the Treaty of Vienna clauses and Balkan crisis triggers to score 100%.";
    } else {
      finalMsg.textContent = "Review the core timelines and visual summaries above, then re-attempt the quiz.";
    }
  }

  restartBtn.onclick = () => {
    userAnswers = new Array(quizData.length).fill(null);
    currentIdx = 0;
    resultBox.style.display = "none";
    quizBox.style.display = "block";
    renderQuestion(0);
  };

  renderQuestion(0);
})();
</script>

